<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  This app does not make HTTPS requests of its own: it provisions a Termux environment whose tools
  (curl, apt, python) verify TLS using the Termux CA bundle at $PREFIX/etc/tls/cert.pem.

  On corporate networks that perform TLS interception, the organization's root CA is normally
  installed as a *user* certificate on Android. Apps targeting API 24+ (this app targets 28) do
  not trust user-added CAs unless they are declared here. We therefore opt in to user CAs so that
  setup can enumerate the trust anchors the device already accepts and mirror them into the Termux
  bundle — otherwise curl/apt/python inside Termux would reject every HTTPS download even though
  the browser works.

  Note: the app itself opens no sockets to remote hosts, so trusting user CAs here has no effect
  beyond letting setup copy them across.

   The chat UI speaks plain HTTP to the headless `opencode serve` process that runs inside the
   Debian container. proot does not isolate the network namespace, so that server is reachable on
   loopback. A paired computer is also reached over plain HTTP, on a private LAN address chosen by
   the user and protected by the password from its QR link. Android's network security config
   cannot express "cleartext on the local network only" — domain rules cannot cover arbitrary
   private IPs — so cleartext is allowed here. No other host is contacted by this app's own network
   code; provisioning downloads are made by curl/apt inside the container and verify TLS there.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
            <certificates src="user" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
